---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import Figure from "../../components/Figure.astro";
import RobotLogicLab from "../../components/RobotLogicLab.astro";
import EvidenceStrip from "../../components/EvidenceStrip.astro";
import { projects } from "../../data/projects";

const project = projects.find((p) => p.id === "eaglets")!;

/**
 * Kit generations. Verified against the program's own hardware notes.
 * V5 is deliberately marked uncertain rather than filled in.
 */
const kits = [
  {
    v: "V1",
    title: "First working platform",
    image: "/img/eaglets/evolution/v1.jpg",
    alt: "The first Eaglets robot kit, with a breadboard controller, ultrasonic sensor, and exposed wiring on a black chassis.",
    detail:
      "The proof that the whole idea was viable: a younger student could build and program it inside a single session.",
  },
  {
    v: "V2",
    title: "Assembly simplified",
    image: "/img/eaglets/evolution/v2.jpg",
    alt: "The second Eaglets robot kit viewed from above, with a Raspberry Pi Pico and motor driver mounted on a wooden chassis.",
    detail:
      "Rebuilt around the exact steps where students got stuck in the early camps.",
  },
  {
    v: "V3.2",
    title: "Sensors added",
    image: "/img/eaglets/evolution/v3-2.jpg",
    alt: "Eaglets kit version 3.2 with a wooden chassis, sensors, motors, battery, and hand-wired electronics.",
    detail:
      "An ultrasonic distance sensor and a photoresistor. This is where students start writing conditions, like turning when a wall gets close, instead of fixed sequences.",
  },
  {
    v: "V3.6",
    title: "2025 camp season",
    image: "/img/eaglets/evolution/v3-6.jpg",
    alt: "Eaglets kit version 3.6 beside its laser-cut wooden lid, showing the Pico controller, sensors, and wiring.",
    detail:
      "The version that ran the season, with the revision silkscreened onto the chassis so a kit in a bin is identifiable years later.",
  },
  {
    v: "2025 field",
    title: "Breadboard variants",
    detail:
      "The PCBs broke mid-season with camps already booked across Texas. The team rebuilt on breadboards and kept teaching. The controller and motor drive changed three times as the season went on.",
    flag: true,
  },
  {
    v: "V4",
    title: "First designed board",
    detail:
      "The first generation with a proper KiCad design and a schematic on file, rather than a layout that existed mainly in the head of whoever built it.",
  },
  {
    v: "V6",
    title: "Under active development",
    detail:
      "The generation being prepared for testing and summer 2027 production. It isn't finished yet.",
  },
];
---

<Base
  title="Eaglets Robotics, Jamal Mammadzada"
  description="A case study on Eaglets: custom robot kits and curriculum that students in grades 3–8 can build, program, and debug in a single camp session."
  progress={true}
>
  <article>
    <header class="wrap case-head">
      <p class="mono">
        <a href="/work/">Work</a> / Case study {project.index}
      </p>
      <h1 class="h-xl case-title">Eaglets Robotics</h1>
      <p class="lede narrow case-lede">
        A student-run program that teaches free robotics camps for grades 3–8,
        using kits the club has developed over several generations. I run the
        program. I did not design the kits, and this page is mostly about the
        hard part: getting a room full of ten-year-olds to
        finish a working robot and understand what they just did.
      </p>

      <dl class="meta-grid case-meta">
        {
          project.meta.map((m) => (
            <div>
              <dt>{m.label}</dt>
              <dd>{m.value}</dd>
            </div>
          ))
        }
        <div>
          <dt>Involved since</dt>
          <dd>2024</dd>
        </div>
      </dl>

      <p class="note case-note">
        Eaglets is a team effort across a rotating group of student officers,
        and the kit lineage below predates me. Where this page says "the program"
        or "the team," that is not me. My own responsibilities are camps,
        curriculum, officer training, and keeping the sessions free.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="wrap case-proof">
      <EvidenceStrip
        label="Eaglets program evidence"
        items={[
          { value: "Grades 3–8", label: "Student audience", detail: "Build, program, and debug in one session" },
          { value: "6", label: "Design generations", detail: "Revised from real camp failures" },
          { value: "Free camps", label: "Program model", detail: "Hardware, curriculum, and volunteers together" },
          { value: "2024–present", label: "Leadership", detail: "Camp coordinator to president" },
        ]}
      />
    </div>

    <section class="wrap section narrow" aria-labelledby="problem">
      <h2 class="h-lg sec-title" id="problem">The engineering problem</h2>

      <p>
        Off-the-shelf educational robots solve the wrong problem. The good ones
        are expensive enough that a free camp can't buy a classroom set. The
        cheap ones are either toys that don't teach programming, or bare
        components that lose a beginner before anything moves.
      </p>

      <p>
        So the program builds its own. That turns a teaching problem into a
        hardware and software problem with unusual constraints. The kit has to be
        cheap enough to buy in quantity and rugged enough to be assembled,
        disassembled, and dropped by a rotating cast of elementary schoolers. It
        has to go from a box of parts to a moving robot within a session, because
        a camp doesn't get a second day to recover a lost room.
      </p>

      <p>
        And the code a nine-year-old writes on day one has to do something
        visible, without hiding so much that they haven't learned anything. That
        last constraint is the one that shapes the software.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="wrap kit-photo" aria-label="Eaglets kit photograph">
      <Figure
        src="/img/eaglets/kit-deck-and-chassis.jpg"
        alt="An Eaglets robot kit with its engraved top deck lifted off, showing a Raspberry Pi Pico, a 9V battery, an ultrasonic sensor, LEDs, and colour-coded jumper wiring on a laser-cut plywood chassis."
        label="Plate 01 — current kit, deck removed"
        caption="The deck lifts off so the wiring underneath is visible during a build. Raspberry Pi Pico, TT gearmotors, an ultrasonic sensor at the front, and the club's chick mascot engraved into the top plate."
        width={782}
        height={626}
      />

      <Figure
        src="/img/eaglets/camp-kit-debug.jpg"
        alt="Jamal at a camp table, one hand on a part-built Eaglets robot and the other on a laptop trackpad, while a student leans in to watch."
        label="Plate 02 — camp session"
        caption="The part of the job that's mine: sitting with a build that will not run and working out which of the nine wires is in the wrong header."
        width={1004}
        height={710}
      />
    </section>

    <section class="wrap section" aria-labelledby="lineage">
      <h2 class="h-lg sec-title narrow" id="lineage">Six generations of kit</h2>

      <p class="narrow lineage-intro">
        Each version came out of something that went wrong at a camp. Select a
        generation to read what changed.
      </p>

      <div class="lineage" id="kit-lineage">
        <div class="lineage-track" role="list">
          {
            kits.map((k, i) => (
              <button
                type="button"
                role="listitem"
                class:list={["kit", { "is-active": i === 0, "is-flag": k.flag }]}
                data-kit={i}
                aria-expanded={i === 0 ? "true" : "false"}
                aria-controls="kit-detail"
              >
                <span class="kit-v mono">{k.v}</span>
                <span class="kit-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span>
              </button>
            ))
          }
        </div>

        <div class="kit-detail" id="kit-detail" aria-live="polite">
          <figure class="kit-evidence" data-kit-figure>
            <img
              src={kits[0].image}
              alt={kits[0].alt}
              width="900"
              height="904"
              loading="lazy"
              data-kit-image
            />
            <figcaption class="mono" data-kit-caption>{kits[0].v} · repository photograph</figcaption>
          </figure>
          <div>
            <h3 class="h-sm" data-kit-title>{kits[0].title}</h3>
            <p data-kit-detail>{kits[0].detail}</p>
            <p class="mono kit-no-photo" data-kit-no-photo hidden>
              No verified photograph is linked to this revision.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="narrow">
        <p class="note">
          The V5 board appears in a bill of materials, but its design files
          couldn't be located. Either it lives somewhere outside the shared drive, or
          kits were built from something that was never checked in. Rather than
          guess, it's left out of the lineage above.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="wrap section narrow" aria-labelledby="failure">
      <h2 class="h-lg sec-title" id="failure">The summer the boards broke</h2>

      <p>
        Mid-season in 2025, with camps already booked across Texas, the PCBs
        failed. Cancelling wasn't an option, because those camps were the program.
      </p>

      <p>
        The team rebuilt on breadboards and kept going, and the design changed
        under them three times as the season ran. The first camps drove motors
        through transistors, which turned out to be the weak point and became a
        motor driver. The controller moved to Arduino and back to Raspberry Pi
        Pico once it could be supported again. The LEDs got cut, because a Pico
        takes up most of a breadboard and something had to go.
      </p>

      <p>
        It would be easy to tell the breadboard switch as a teaching decision:
        students see the circuit, so wiring becomes part of the lesson. That
        isn't what happened. The boards broke, the team improvised under a
        deadline, and the camps ran anyway.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="wrap section" aria-labelledby="software">
      <h2 class="h-lg sec-title narrow" id="software">Software a beginner can get through</h2>

      <p class="narrow">
        I've worked on the beginner-facing libraries and taught Python and C++ at
        camps. The design question that never goes away is how much to hide.
      </p>

      <RobotLogicLab />

      <Figure
        src="/img/jamal-teaching.jpg"
        alt="Jamal speaking from a podium at an Eaglets camp session, with the program's slide projected behind him."
        label="Plate 03"
        caption="Running a session at an Eaglets camp. Photographs from camps are cropped to exclude students."
        width={940}
        height={608}
      />

      <div class="narrow">
        <p>
          Wrap too little and a beginner spends the session on pin configuration
          and PWM and never gets the robot to move. Wrap too much and you've
          built a toy where <code>go_forward()</code> is the whole lesson, and the
          student succeeds without understanding anything transferable.
        </p>

        <p>
          The line we've settled on: hide the register-level setup, expose the
          logic. A student writes the condition that decides what happens when
          the distance sensor reads under some threshold. They don't write the
          ultrasonic timing code, but the decision is theirs, and it's a real
          one. Sensors are what make that possible, and they're usually where
          students stop copying code and start debugging it.
        </p>

        <details class="tech">
          <summary>Debugging teaches the most</summary>
          <p>
            The moment worth engineering for is when a student's robot does
            something they didn't intend. That's when they have to reason about
            what the code actually says versus what they meant, which is the
            skill that carries.
          </p>
          <p>
            It also means the kit has to fail in ways a beginner can see. A robot
            that stops silently teaches nothing. One that visibly turns the wrong
            way gives a student something to chase.
          </p>
        </details>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="wrap section narrow" aria-labelledby="leading">
      <h2 class="h-lg sec-title" id="leading">Running the program</h2>

      <p>
        Eaglets turns over its entire leadership every year, which is the defining
        constraint on how it's run. Every officer group inherits an organization
        from people who have graduated, and anything that only exists in
        someone's head is lost at the handoff.
      </p>

      <p>What I'm responsible for right now:</p>

      <ul class="resp-list" role="list">
        <li>
          <strong>Rebuilding the 2026–27 team.</strong> Recruiting into open
          officer roles and training new officers, with the assumption that
          they'll arrive with no context.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>V6 development.</strong> Preparing the current kit generation
          for testing and summer 2027 production. V6 is not production-ready yet.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Season planning.</strong> Building the curriculum, logistics,
          and volunteer capacity needed for the summer 2027 program.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Grants and partnerships.</strong> Funding and the
          organizational relationships that keep camps free.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Technical continuity.</strong> Making sure design decisions are
          written down where the next officer team will find them, which is the
          only thing that stops the program relearning the same lessons.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p class="resp-after">
        None of that is what I expected the job to be. Most of the technical work
        turns out to be documentation and handoff. An organization that forgets why
        the boards broke will design the same failure again.
      </p>
    </section>

    <nav class="wrap section case-next" aria-label="More work">
      <a href="/work/cryptography/" class="next-link">
        <span class="mono">Next case study</span>
        <span class="h-md">Practical Cryptography →</span>
      </a>
    </nav>
  </article>
</Base>

<script is:inline define:vars={{ kits }}>
  (function () {
    const root = document.getElementById("kit-lineage");
    if (!root) return;

    const title = root.querySelector("[data-kit-title]");
    const detail = root.querySelector("[data-kit-detail]");
    const figure = root.querySelector("[data-kit-figure]");
    const image = root.querySelector("[data-kit-image]");
    const caption = root.querySelector("[data-kit-caption]");
    const noPhoto = root.querySelector("[data-kit-no-photo]");
    const buttons = root.querySelectorAll("[data-kit]");

    function select(i) {
      const k = kits[i];
      if (!k) return;

      buttons.forEach((b, j) => {
        const on = i === j;
        b.classList.toggle("is-active", on);
        b.setAttribute("aria-expanded", String(on));
      });

      if (title) title.textContent = k.title;
      if (detail) detail.textContent = k.detail;
      if (k.image && image && figure) {
        image.src = k.image;
        image.alt = k.alt || "";
        if (caption) caption.textContent = `${k.v} · repository photograph`;
        figure.hidden = false;
        if (noPhoto) noPhoto.hidden = true;
      } else {
        if (figure) figure.hidden = true;
        if (noPhoto) noPhoto.hidden = false;
      }
    }

    buttons.forEach((b, i) => {
      b.addEventListener("click", () => select(i));
      b.addEventListener("focus", () => select(i));
    });
  })();
</script>

<style>
  .case-head {
    padding-top: clamp(2.5rem, 6vw, 4.5rem);
  }

  .case-head > .mono {
    margin-bottom: 1.4rem;
  }

  .case-title {
    margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
  }

  .case-lede {
    margin-bottom: 2.5rem;
  }

  .case-meta {
    max-width: 34rem;
    margin-bottom: 1.75rem;
  }

  .case-note {
    max-width: 60ch;
  }

  .case-proof {
    margin-top: clamp(2.5rem, 6vw, 4.5rem);
  }

  .sec-title {
    margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
  }

  .lineage-intro {
    margin-bottom: 2rem;
  }

  .kit-photo {
    padding-top: clamp(0.5rem, 2vw, 1.25rem);
  }

  .kit-photo :global(.fig) {
    max-width: 52rem;
  }

  .lineage {
    border: 1px solid var(--rule);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    background: var(--paper-2);
    padding: 1.4rem 1.2rem 1.2rem;
    margin-bottom: 2rem;
  }

  .lineage-track {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(7, 1fr);
    gap: 0.25rem;
    position: relative;
    padding-bottom: 1.3rem;
  }

  /* The timeline rule the dots sit on. */
  .lineage-track::after {
    content: "";
    position: absolute;
    left: 0;
    right: 0;
    bottom: 4px;
    height: 1px;
    background: var(--rule);
  }

  .kit {
    display: grid;
    justify-items: start;
    gap: 0.55rem;
    background: transparent;
    border: 0;
    padding: 0 0 0.1rem;
    cursor: pointer;
    font: inherit;
    color: var(--ink-3);
    position: relative;
    z-index: 1;
    text-align: left;
    transition: color 0.16s ease;
  }

  .kit:hover {
    color: var(--ink);
  }

  .kit.is-active {
    color: var(--accent);
  }

  .kit-v {
    font-size: var(--fs-label);
    color: inherit;
  }

  .kit-dot {
    width: 9px;
    height: 9px;
    border-radius: 50%;
    border: 1.5px solid currentColor;
    background: var(--paper-2);
    transition: background 0.16s ease;
  }

  .kit.is-active .kit-dot {
    background: var(--accent);
  }

  .kit.is-flag .kit-dot {
    border-style: dashed;
  }

  .kit-detail {
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
    padding-top: 1rem;
    min-height: 13rem;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(180px, 0.7fr) minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: clamp(1rem, 3vw, 2rem);
    align-items: start;
  }

  .kit-evidence {
    margin: 0;
  }

  .kit-evidence img {
    width: 100%;
    aspect-ratio: 4 / 3;
    object-fit: cover;
    border: 1px solid var(--rule);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
  }

  .kit-evidence figcaption {
    margin-top: 0.55rem;
    font-size: var(--fs-label);
  }

  .kit-detail h3 {
    margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
  }

  .kit-detail p {
    color: var(--ink-2);
    font-size: var(--fs-ui);
    margin: 0;
  }

  .kit-detail .kit-no-photo {
    margin-top: 1rem;
    font-size: var(--fs-label);
  }

  code {
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: 0.86em;
    background: var(--accent-wash);
    padding: 0.1em 0.35em;
    border-radius: var(--radius);
  }

  .resp-list {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 1.25rem 0 0;
    padding: 0;
    display: grid;
    gap: 1rem;
  }

  .resp-list li {
    border-left: 2px solid var(--rule);
    padding-left: 1rem;
    color: var(--ink-2);
    font-size: var(--fs-ui);
  }

  .resp-list strong {
    color: var(--ink);
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .resp-after {
    margin-top: 1.75rem;
  }

  .case-next {
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }

  .next-link {
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    text-decoration: none;
    color: inherit;
    padding: 1.5rem 0;
  }

  .next-link .h-md {
    transition: color 0.16s ease;
  }

  .next-link:hover .h-md {
    color: var(--accent);
  }

  @media (max-width: 700px) {
    .lineage-track {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(4, 1fr);
      gap: 1rem 0.4rem;
    }

    .lineage-track::after {
      display: none;
    }

    .kit-detail {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
  }
</style>
